---
term: Dissolution
section: life-cycle
order: 60
definition: The orderly ending of a corporation under the procedure its charter defines.
seeAlso:
  - exit
  - charter-formation
---
A dissolution procedure states what happens to the corporation's assets, commons, obligations, members, and subordinate corporations. Writing it in advance keeps the end of a corporation as predictable as its creation.

A corporation is worth keeping while it combines its members' agreements into more influence than they would have apart. When a charter has extracted all the agreement its members can reconcile, dissolution lets them stop maintaining a system that no longer achieves that.
